WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.133
The agent that deleted production data.

2
00:00:03.433 --> 00:00:05.044
Four thousand rows vanished.

3
00:00:05.044 --> 00:00:06.367
What really went wrong?

4
00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:12.433
Your internal ops agent has a run SQL tool to answer data questions.

5
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:16.167
A user asks it to clean up the duplicate customer records.

6
00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:18.348
It writes and executes a delete.

7
00:00:18.348 --> 00:00:20.700
Four thousand two hundred rows are gone.

8
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.700
Backups are six hours old, and your manager asks if we can still use agents.

9
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.233
First, the first hour.

10
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:31.015
Disable the tool, not the whole agent.

11
00:00:31.015 --> 00:00:31.733
Be precise.

12
00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:33.867
Restore the backup into staging.

13
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.900
Rebuild the six hour gap from application logs.

14
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.800
Pull the agent's trajectory.

15
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.667
And communicate scope early.

16
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:43.367
Now name the real failure.

17
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:45.921
It isn't that the model made a mistake.

18
00:00:45.921 --> 00:00:48.233
Models will do anything the tool allows.

19
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:56.451
The failure is that an irreversible capability was exposed with no permission boundary, no human checkpoint, and no dry run.

20
00:00:56.451 --> 00:00:58.367
That's an architecture defect.

21
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:01.767
Here is the redesign, layer by layer.

22
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:04.476
The SQL tool is read-only by default.

23
00:01:04.476 --> 00:01:07.667
Writes live behind a separate tool with approval.

24
00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:11.000
Writes preview the rows they'd touch and wait for a human.

25
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.133
The database role simply can't delete or drop.

26
00:01:14.433 --> 00:01:16.767
Large statements escalate no matter what.

27
00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:21.067
And soft deletes plus an audit log make every mistake recoverable.

28
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:23.867
So what do you tell your manager?

29
00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:25.474
Don't blame the model.

30
00:01:25.474 --> 00:01:27.733
The cause was unreviewed write access.

31
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:30.278
And don't ask the model to be careful.

32
00:01:30.278 --> 00:01:31.400
Enforce it in code.

33
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.500
Yes, we can use agents, with the controls we'd give a new intern.

34
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.967
The classic trap is fixing it in the prompt.

35
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:41.576
A prompt is not a security boundary.

36
00:01:41.576 --> 00:01:44.333
Enforce it in code and permissions, always.

37
00:01:45.533 --> 00:01:46.312
Let's recap.

38
00:01:46.312 --> 00:01:49.233
In hour one, disable the tool, not the agent.

39
00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:52.467
The failure was an ungated, irreversible action.

40
00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:56.700
Layer read-only access, dry runs, least privilege, and caps.

41
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.049
Enforce it in code, not prompts.

42
00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:01.033
Next up: it worked in the demo.
